<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bank of Saint George</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1407">1407</a>–<a href="/years/1805">1805</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Bank of San Giorgio, Casa di San Giorgio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bank of Saint George, or Casa di San Giorgio, was a financial institution of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, founded in 1407 to consolidate and manage the state's public debt. It collected the taxes assigned to its creditors and in the 15th century took over the administration of Genoese colonies such as Famagusta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>. One of the oldest banks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, it was wound up in 1805.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bank-of-saint-george.jpg" alt="Bank of Saint George" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jensens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palazzo_San_Georgio_Genova_W.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The bank was founded on 23 April 1407 to consolidate the public debt of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, which had grown with the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> for dominance in trade and finance. Its main task was to manage the San Giorgio shares, the luoghi held by the state's creditors. Its formal name was the Casa delle compere e dei banchi di San Giorgio, and it was also called the Ufficio di San Giorgio or simply the Banco. Its seat was the Palazzo San Giorgio, built in the 13th century on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guglielmo-boccanegra">Guglielmo Boccanegra</a>, uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simone-boccanegra">Simone Boccanegra</a>, the first Doge of Genoa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The bank needed frequent injections of money to support the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the republic's weak finances. By 1445 it had suspended its business with the public to serve the Genoese state alone, and it reopened to the public only in 1530. In 1453 the republic handed the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, Gazaria and other possessions to its officials, though over the rest of the century the republic took many of them back.</p><p>In the 15th and 16th centuries the bank lent large sums to rulers across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Ferdinand and Isabella held accounts there, as did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>, who wrote to its governors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> on 2 April 1502, before his fourth voyage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> was heavily in its debt for much of his reign. In the 17th century it became heavily involved in maritime trade and for a time competed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> he suppressed independent banks, and the bank closed in 1805.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Writers on government long cited the bank. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a>, in his Florentine Histories of 1532, saw in it liberty and tyranny, integrity and corruption within one community. Joseph Addison, travelling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1701, noted that its managers had been called a second kind of senate but judged it a check on the aristocracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-spirit-of-the-laws">The Spirit of Law</a> (1748) wrote that its administration gave the people a certain influence in the government, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hume">David Hume</a> held that while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> was full of sedition the bank was conducted for several ages with the utmost integrity and wisdom.</p><p>Thomas Babington Macaulay, in The History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> (1848), recalled it with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-amsterdam">Bank of Amsterdam</a> as one of the two public banks held up as models when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-england">Bank of England</a> was established in 1694. On 11 June 1857 the old lock of its vault was presented to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> historical society.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bank of Saint George” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Bank of Saint George</b>, a creditors’ bank founded in 1407, governed the island of Corsica on the republic’s behalf for over a century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Genoa governed Famagusta through a podesta and officials, and from 1447 through the <b>Bank of Saint George</b>, which ran it as a business.</blockquote><a href="/state/genoese-famagusta">Genoese Famagusta</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1453 Genoa handed the colonies to the <b>Bank of San Giorgio</b>, as the Ottoman Empire controlled the straits.</blockquote><a href="/state/genoese-gazaria">Genoese Gazaria</a></li></ul></section>
